This repository contains the artifact for the study presented on the paper *AI-related Vulnerabilities within CVEs: Are We Ready Yet? A Study of Vulnerability Disclosure in AI Products* (Accepted at AISec'25 - ACM CCS 2025).
Please, if you use this code or dataset, consider citing our paper with:

```bibtex
@inproceedings{maugeri2025airelatedvulnerabilities,
    title={AI-related Vulnerabilities within CVEs: Are We Ready Yet? A Study of Vulnerability Disclosure in AI Products},
    author={Maugeri, Marcello and Castiglione, Gianpietro and Raciti, Mario and Bella, Giampaolo},
    booktitle={Proceedings of the 2025 Workshop on Artificial Intelligence and Security},
    year={2025},
    isbn = {9798400718953},
    publisher = {Association for Computing Machinery},
    address = {New York, NY, USA},
    url = {https://doi.org/10.1145/3733799.3762969},
    doi = {10.1145/3733799.3762969},
    keywords={Adversarial AI, Machine Learning Security, Vulnerability Management, CVE, NIST AI 100-2e2025, AI Supply Chain, Vulnerability Disclosure},
    series = {AISec '25}
}
```

*IMPORTANT:* Note that the paper is not published yet, then the current DOI is not accessible yet.

## Project Overview

- *agent*: Contains the agent implementation for CVE analysis (based on Google ADK)
- *dataset*: Contains the dataset used for the evaluation.
- *scripts*: Contains scripts for running the analysis workflow and plotting results.
- *plots*: Contains the generated plots from the analysis.
- *assets*: Contains images and other assets used in the project.

## Agent Workflow
**AI CVE Analyser** is a **multi-agent system** designed to analyse CVEs and classify their relevance to AI.
The agentic workflow is orchestrated using the [Google ADK](https://github.com/google/adk) framework and is composed of two main agents designed in an actor-critic pattern:

- **CVE Classifier Agent**:  
  This agent receives CVE metadata and determines whether the vulnerability is related to AI.
  It classifies each CVE into one of three categories:
  - *Non-AI*: Traditional software vulnerabilities not related to AI.
  - *AI-Supply-Chain*: The CVE affects the supply chain of AI systems, without adversarial AI impact.
  - *Adversarial-AI*: The CVE is directly related to adversarial attacks or vulnerabilities in AI/ML models.

- **NIST Mapper Agent**:  
  If a CVE is classified as *Adversarial-AI*, this agent maps the CVE to the appropriate category in the [NIST AI 100-2e2025 Taxonomy](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-2e2025.pdf).

The picture below represents the workflow of the multi-agent system.
![assets/workflow.jpg](assets/workflow.jpg)

## Getting Started

### 1. Download the CVE List
This project relies on the [CVE List](https://github.com/CVEProject/cvelistV5) dataset which contains all public CVEs records.
For the replication of the paper study, please download this [ZIP file](https://github.com/CVEProject/cvelistV5/releases/download/cve_2025-06-01_0000Z/2025-05-31_all_CVEs_at_midnight.zip.zip), which contains all public CVEs record until the midnight of June, 01 2025.
You may want to replicate the experiments with new CVEs as they are published, in that case, simply download the release you prefer [here](https://github.com/CVEProject/cvelistV5/releases/).
Once done, include the `cves` folder in the root of this project.

### 2. Install Python Requirements
```bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
cd agent
POETRY_VIRTUALENVS_CREATE=false poetry install
```

### 3. Run the agentic workflow
```bash
EXPORT OPENAI_API_KEY=<your_openai_api_key>
python scripts/classify_cves.py --year <year> --model <model>
```
And wait for the processing to complete (You can stop it anytime, it will resume from the last processed CVE).

#### Use another model
This project is based on *Google ADK* and accepts *OpenAI* models thanks to the *LiteLLM* interface.
To use other providers, change `DEFAULT_LITELLM_API_BASE` in config.py. 

## Replicate the study
To replicate the study proposed in the paper, run the agentic workflow with the default model for years between 2021 and 2025.
This will generate the dataset folder (note that you need to delete the `dataset` folder if it already exists, otherwise the workflow will skip any existing CVEs).

### Generate plots
Most of the plots presented in the paper can be generated using the following command:
```bash
python scripts/plot_results.py --year_range <default: 2021-2025> --model <default: openai_gpt-4o-mini> --input_csv_dir <default: ./dataset> --cve_json_base_dir <default: ./cves> --output_plot_dir <default: ./plots> --output_format <default: jpeg>
```
They will be saved in the `./plots` directory.

### Generate the stratified sample to perform the (partial) validation
To validate the classification model, we generated a stratified sample from the dataset:
```bash
python perform_stratified_sampling.py --year_range <default: 2021-2025> --model <default: openai_gpt-4o-mini> --cve_json_base_dir <default: ./cves> --input_csv_dir <default: ./dataset> --output_csv_dir <default: ./dataset>
```
More details can be found in the paper.

### Perform the manual validation
As manual validation is a tedious process, we developed a simple Streamlit app to assist with this task.
```bash
streamlit run scripts/validation_app.py -- --input_csv <default: dataset/Stratified_Sample_2021-2025_openai_gpt-4o-mini_10_margin.csv> --output_csv <default: dataset/manual_validation.csv>
```
This will open on a browser and show a window like the one below:
![assets/validation_app.png](assets/validation_app.png)
The app shows the classification results for each CVE in the sample which can be accepted or manually classified.
This will create/update the `dataset/manual_validation.csv` file with the validation results.

### Print and plot the validation results
Finally, we can print and plot the validation results using the following command:
```bash
python scripts/print_validation_results.py --input_csv dataset/manual_validation.csv --output_dir ./plots
```
The confusion matrix will be saved in the output directory and this completes the validation process.

## Contributing

We are open to contributions, including refactoring, new approaches, additional features and extended study.
Feel free to open a pull request or, if you are willing to discuss research-related topics, please reach out to us by e-mail.
We are actively working on this project to extend this study, then it is advisable to reach out if you are interested in collaborating.

